% 48-self-hosting-lexer.weft — Stage 8b: a tokenizer written in C,
% compiled by kcc, and checked against clang.

\chapter{Self-Hosting: A Tokenizer in C}
\label{ch:self-hosting-lexer}

The arena of Chapter~\ref{ch:self-hosting-arena} was small enough to
drop straight back into the compiler.  A tokenizer is a larger test: it
branches heavily, keeps mutable cursor state, indexes tables, and builds
tokens through pointers.  Rather than swap it into \kcc{}'s own
front-end---which would entangle it with the parser and preprocessor---%
this chapter takes the tokenizer as a self-contained \emph{program}: a
faithful C re-implementation of the scanning logic of
Chapter~\ref{ch:lexer}, using the same token codes, that tokenizes
embedded source buffers and asserts the result.  \kcc{} compiles it;
its own assertions and a differential comparison against \texttt{clang}
judge it.

Writing it flushed out two real defects in \kcc{}, both triggered only
by a function of this size and shape, and both now fixed:

\begin{itemize}
\item The code generator's basic-block label table was a fixed
      64-slot, open-addressed hash with no resize.  A function with more
      than that many blocks---\texttt{lex\_next} has well over sixty---%
      overflowed it, and \texttt{ht\_put} spun forever.  The table is
      now sized to the block count (\S\ref{sec:preregister-globals}
      neighbourhood, in \texttt{codegen\_func}).
\item The address-of operator assumed its operand was always a bare
      identifier.  \texttt{\&tokens[n]}---a subscript---was mis-read as a
      name and lowered to a bogus global reference.  \texttt{\&} now
      dispatches on operand shape.
\end{itemize}

That a 250-line program surfaced two codegen bugs is the point of
Stage~8b: real code exercises feature \emph{combinations} the unit
tests never reach.

\section{Token Codes}

The codes match Chapter~\ref{ch:lexer} exactly, so a token stream from
this C lexer is directly comparable to \kcc{}'s own.  Only the subset
the tests reference is named; the rest share the same numbering.

@l c
@d Selfhost: lexer tokens @{
#define TOK_EOF        0
#define TOK_IDENT      1
#define TOK_NUMBER     2
#define TOK_CHAR_LIT   3
#define TOK_STRING_LIT 4
#define TOK_UNKNOWN    5

#define TOK_INT       27
#define TOK_RETURN    31
#define TOK_IF        25
#define TOK_ELSE      19
#define TOK_WHILE     43
#define TOK_FOR       23
#define TOK_VOID      41
#define TOK_CHAR      13
#define TOK_STRUCT    36

#define TOK_LPAREN    60
#define TOK_RPAREN    61
#define TOK_LBRACE    62
#define TOK_RBRACE    63
#define TOK_SEMI      66
#define TOK_STAR      73
#define TOK_PLUS      74
#define TOK_ARROW     69
#define TOK_INC       70
#define TOK_EQ        86
#define TOK_ASSIGN    94
#define TOK_LE        84
#define TOK_ELLIPSIS 107

struct Token {
    int type;
    int start;
    int len;
    int line;
    int col;
};
@}

\section{State and the Keyword Table}

Like the assembly lexer, cursor state lives in globals.  The keyword
table is two parallel arrays---names and codes---initialised with
brace-enclosed lists.  The name array is an array of \texttt{char~*}
seeded with string literals, which is precisely the pointer-array
initializer form Stage~8a added; getting it wrong would surface as a
mis-classified keyword.

@l c
@d Selfhost: lexer state @{

const char *g_src;
int g_pos;
int g_line;
int g_line_start;

#define NKW 9
char *kw_names[NKW] = { "int", "return", "if", "else", "while",
                        "for", "void", "char", "struct" };
int   kw_codes[NKW] = { TOK_INT, TOK_RETURN, TOK_IF, TOK_ELSE, TOK_WHILE,
                        TOK_FOR, TOK_VOID, TOK_CHAR, TOK_STRUCT };
@}

\section{Character Classes and Keyword Lookup}

Character classification is expressed as range tests rather than a
lookup table---simpler, and equally within \kcc{}'s reach.  A keyword is
recognised by scanning the parallel arrays: the candidate must match the
identifier text and end exactly at its length, so \texttt{intx} is an
identifier, not \texttt{int} followed by \texttt{x}.

@l c
@d Selfhost: lexer classify @{

int is_alpha(int c) { return (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') || (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z') || c == '_'; }
int is_digit(int c) { return c >= '0' && c <= '9'; }
int is_alnum(int c) { return is_alpha(c) || is_digit(c); }
int is_space(int c) { return c == ' ' || c == '\t' || c == '\n' || c == '\r'; }

int kw_match(const char *kw, const char *s, int len) {
    int i = 0;
    while (i < len) {
        if (kw[i] == 0) return 0;
        if (kw[i] != s[i]) return 0;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return kw[len] == 0;
}

int lookup_kw(const char *s, int len) {
    int i = 0;
    while (i < NKW) {
        if (kw_match(kw_names[i], s, len)) return kw_codes[i];
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return TOK_IDENT;
}
@}

\section{The Cursor}

\texttt{peek} reads the current byte; \texttt{advance} steps forward,
tracking line and column by remembering where each line began.
\texttt{skip\_trivia} consumes whitespace and both comment forms before
each token.

@l c
@d Selfhost: lexer cursor @{

int peek(void) { return g_src[g_pos]; }

void advance(void) {
    if (g_src[g_pos] == '\n') {
        g_line = g_line + 1;
        g_line_start = g_pos + 1;
    }
    g_pos = g_pos + 1;
}

void skip_trivia(void) {
    while (peek() != 0) {
        int c = peek();
        if (is_space(c)) { advance(); }
        else if (c == '/' && g_src[g_pos + 1] == '/') {
            while (peek() != 0 && peek() != '\n') advance();
        }
        else if (c == '/' && g_src[g_pos + 1] == '*') {
            advance(); advance();
            while (peek() != 0) {
                if (peek() == '*' && g_src[g_pos + 1] == '/') { advance(); advance(); return; }
                advance();
            }
        }
        else { return; }
    }
}
@}

\section{Scanning One Token}

\texttt{lex\_next} records the start position, line, and column, then
classifies by first character: identifier or keyword, pp-number,
string, character constant, or a punctuator scanned with maximal
munch---three-character \texttt{...} first, then the two-character
operators, then the single-character remainder.  The result is written
through the caller's \texttt{Token} pointer.

@l c
@d Selfhost: lexer next @{

int lex_next(struct Token *t) {
    skip_trivia();
    int start = g_pos;
    int col = start - g_line_start + 1;
    int line = g_line;
    int c = peek();

    t->start = start;
    t->line = line;
    t->col = col;

    if (c == 0) { t->type = TOK_EOF; t->len = 0; return TOK_EOF; }

    if (is_alpha(c)) {
        while (is_alnum(peek())) advance();
        int len = g_pos - start;
        t->len = len;
        t->type = lookup_kw(g_src + start, len);
        return t->type;
    }
    if (is_digit(c)) {
        while (is_alnum(peek()) || peek() == '.') advance();
        t->len = g_pos - start;
        t->type = TOK_NUMBER;
        return TOK_NUMBER;
    }
    if (c == '"') {
        advance();
        while (peek() != 0 && peek() != '"') {
            if (peek() == '\\') advance();
            advance();
        }
        if (peek() == '"') advance();
        t->len = g_pos - start;
        t->type = TOK_STRING_LIT;
        return TOK_STRING_LIT;
    }
    if (c == '\'') {
        advance();
        while (peek() != 0 && peek() != '\'') {
            if (peek() == '\\') advance();
            advance();
        }
        if (peek() == '\'') advance();
        t->len = g_pos - start;
        t->type = TOK_CHAR_LIT;
        return TOK_CHAR_LIT;
    }

    int c1 = g_src[g_pos + 1];
    if (c == '.' && c1 == '.' && g_src[g_pos + 2] == '.') {
        advance(); advance(); advance();
        t->len = 3; t->type = TOK_ELLIPSIS; return TOK_ELLIPSIS;
    }
    if (c == '-' && c1 == '>') { advance(); advance(); t->len = 2; t->type = TOK_ARROW; return TOK_ARROW; }
    if (c == '+' && c1 == '+') { advance(); advance(); t->len = 2; t->type = TOK_INC; return TOK_INC; }
    if (c == '=' && c1 == '=') { advance(); advance(); t->len = 2; t->type = TOK_EQ; return TOK_EQ; }
    if (c == '<' && c1 == '=') { advance(); advance(); t->len = 2; t->type = TOK_LE; return TOK_LE; }

    advance();
    t->len = 1;
    if (c == '(') t->type = TOK_LPAREN;
    else if (c == ')') t->type = TOK_RPAREN;
    else if (c == '{') t->type = TOK_LBRACE;
    else if (c == '}') t->type = TOK_RBRACE;
    else if (c == ';') t->type = TOK_SEMI;
    else if (c == '*') t->type = TOK_STAR;
    else if (c == '+') t->type = TOK_PLUS;
    else if (c == '=') t->type = TOK_ASSIGN;
    else t->type = TOK_UNKNOWN;
    return t->type;
}
@}

\section{Tokenizing a Buffer}

\texttt{lex\_all} resets the cursor and fills an array of tokens until
end of input or the caller's capacity.  Its \texttt{lex\_next(\&out[n])}
call is the one that exposed the address-of defect: \texttt{\&out[n]} is
the address of a subscript on a pointer parameter.

@l c
@d Selfhost: lexer all @{

int lex_all(const char *src, struct Token *out, int max) {
    g_src = src; g_pos = 0; g_line = 1; g_line_start = 0;
    int n = 0;
    while (n < max) {
        int ty = lex_next(&out[n]);
        n = n + 1;
        if (ty == TOK_EOF) return n;
    }
    return n;
}
@}

\section{The Self-Test}

\texttt{main} tokenizes four representative inputs and checks token
counts, types, lengths, and---for the character literal after a
newline---line and column tracking.  It returns~0 only when every check
holds, so the exit status alone certifies the run; a distinct non-zero
code per check pinpoints any failure.

@l c
@d Selfhost: lexer main @{

int main(void) {
    struct Token toks[64];

    int n = lex_all("int main(void){ return 42; }", toks, 64);
    if (n != 11) return 1;
    if (toks[0].type != TOK_INT) return 2;
    if (toks[1].type != TOK_IDENT) return 3;
    if (toks[2].type != TOK_LPAREN) return 4;
    if (toks[3].type != TOK_VOID) return 5;
    if (toks[4].type != TOK_RPAREN) return 6;
    if (toks[5].type != TOK_LBRACE) return 7;
    if (toks[6].type != TOK_RETURN) return 8;
    if (toks[7].type != TOK_NUMBER) return 9;
    if (toks[7].len != 2) return 10;
    if (toks[8].type != TOK_SEMI) return 11;
    if (toks[10].type != TOK_EOF) return 12;

    n = lex_all("a->b ++ x == y // trailing\n", toks, 64);
    if (n != 8) return 13;
    if (toks[1].type != TOK_ARROW) return 14;
    if (toks[3].type != TOK_INC) return 15;
    if (toks[5].type != TOK_EQ) return 16;

    n = lex_all("char *s = \"hi\";\n'x'", toks, 64);
    if (toks[4].type != TOK_STRING_LIT) return 17;
    if (toks[4].len != 4) return 18;
    int i = 0;
    int found = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        if (toks[i].type == TOK_CHAR_LIT) {
            if (toks[i].line != 2) return 19;
            if (toks[i].col != 1) return 20;
            found = 1;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if (found == 0) return 21;

    n = lex_all("f(int a, ...) /* skip */ ;", toks, 64);
    i = 0; found = 0;
    while (i < n) { if (toks[i].type == TOK_ELLIPSIS) found = 1; i = i + 1; }
    if (found == 0) return 22;

    return 0;
}
@}

\section{Assembling the Translation Unit}

@l c
@d Selfhost: lexer @{
@<Selfhost: lexer tokens@>
@<Selfhost: lexer state@>
@<Selfhost: lexer classify@>
@<Selfhost: lexer cursor@>
@<Selfhost: lexer next@>
@<Selfhost: lexer all@>
@<Selfhost: lexer main@>
@}

\section{Verification}
\label{sec:selfhost-lexer-verify}

\texttt{make selfhost-lexer} compiles \texttt{tmp/selfhost/lexer.c} with
\kcc{}, links it freestanding (entry \texttt{\_main}, no libc), and runs
it: exit~0 means every assertion held.  It then compiles the identical
source with \texttt{clang} and confirms the two binaries agree---%
differential testing in miniature, and a preview of Stage~8c.  A match
means \kcc{} generated correct code for a real, branch-heavy,
table-driven program.

@d Selfhost: lexer verify @{
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# make selfhost-lexer — compile the C tokenizer with kcc, self-test, diff clang.
set -euo pipefail

SDK=$(xcrun --show-sdk-path)
SH=tmp/selfhost

echo "[1/2] compiling + running the tokenizer built by kcc"
tmp/kcc -c "$SH/lexer.c" -o "$SH/lexer_kcc.o" >/dev/null
ld "$SH/lexer_kcc.o" -o "$SH/lexer_kcc" -lSystem -syslibroot "$SDK" -e _main -arch arm64
set +e
"$SH/lexer_kcc"; kcc_rc=$?
set -e
echo "      kcc-compiled tokenizer self-test exit: $kcc_rc (expect 0)"

echo "[2/2] differential check against clang"
clang -c "$SH/lexer.c" -o "$SH/lexer_clang.o"
ld "$SH/lexer_clang.o" -o "$SH/lexer_clang" -lSystem -syslibroot "$SDK" -e _main -arch arm64
set +e
"$SH/lexer_clang"; clang_rc=$?
set -e
echo "      clang-compiled tokenizer self-test exit: $clang_rc (expect 0)"

if [ "$kcc_rc" -eq 0 ] && [ "$kcc_rc" -eq "$clang_rc" ]; then
    echo "PASS: kcc and clang agree, tokenizer self-test green"
    exit 0
fi
echo "FAIL: kcc=$kcc_rc clang=$clang_rc"
exit 1
@}
